WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.240
Series from Newton to Einstein. Season one episode one

00:04.440 --> 00:08.920
Have you ever wondered why apples fall straight down and not upwards or sideways?

00:09.360 --> 00:13.520
This simple yet perplexing question has puzzled humankind for centuries

00:14.040 --> 00:16.500
Propelling us into a relentless pursuit of answers

00:17.440 --> 00:24.400
Gravity an invisible force that keeps our feet firmly planted on the ground has been an enigma shrouded in mystery

00:25.040 --> 00:31.920
In the Grand Theatre of the cosmos, it's the unassuming puppeteer silently orchestrating the dance of celestial bodies

00:32.640 --> 00:37.860
Ancient philosophers and early scientists proposed myriad theories to explain this phenomenon

00:38.280 --> 00:41.080
Some believed it to be the work of invisible strings

00:41.600 --> 00:44.560
others surmised ethereal winds were responsible and

00:44.840 --> 00:50.840
For a long while these theories as diverse as they were imaginative formed our understanding of gravity

00:51.200 --> 00:58.560
Yet despite these attempts the true nature of gravity remained elusive a riddle cloaked in the fabric of the universe

00:59.080 --> 01:04.640
It wasn't until Sir Isaac Newton's apple incident that we began to understand gravity a little better

01:05.600 --> 01:07.840
Sir Isaac Newton in the 17th century

01:08.600 --> 01:12.280
Transformed our understanding of gravity with his famous falling apple

01:12.600 --> 01:19.040
This seemingly mundane event sparked a thought in Newton's brilliant mind that birthed his law of universal gravitation

01:19.720 --> 01:23.920
This law simply put states that every particle of matter in the universe

01:24.560 --> 01:30.480
Attracts every other particle with a force that is directly proportional to the product of their masses and

01:31.120 --> 01:34.880
Inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers

01:35.560 --> 01:37.560
This was a groundbreaking revelation

01:38.640 --> 01:44.960
Newton's law of universal gravitation provided a comprehensive explanation for a variety of phenomena

01:45.400 --> 01:48.280
It shed light on why the planets move as they do

01:48.480 --> 01:55.040
Why the tides ebb and flow and even why a thrown ball follows a certain trajectory before falling to the ground?

01:56.280 --> 02:03.520
Newton's contributions to our understanding of gravity were monumental setting the stage for centuries of scientific inquiry

02:04.240 --> 02:10.440
However, this was not the end of the story. There were still some puzzling questions that Newton's laws couldn't answer

02:11.320 --> 02:17.320
Enter Albert Einstein in the 20th century who took our understanding of gravity to a whole new level

02:18.320 --> 02:21.480
Just as Newton had revolutionized science with his law of gravity

02:22.120 --> 02:27.240
Einstein further propelled our comprehension of the universe with his general theory of relativity

02:28.000 --> 02:34.080
This theory put forth in 1915 completely redefined our understanding of gravity

02:34.560 --> 02:41.280
Instead of viewing it as a force as Newton did Einstein portrayed gravity as a consequence of the curvature of space-time

02:41.880 --> 02:43.880
caused by mass and energy

02:44.680 --> 02:51.200
Imagine space-time as a giant rubber sheet if you place a heavy ball on it, it would cause the sheet to sag

02:51.920 --> 02:58.000
Now if you roll a smaller ball near the larger one the smaller ball would naturally fall towards the larger one

02:58.160 --> 03:02.360
Not because of an invisible force pulling it but because of the warped path

03:02.360 --> 03:06.820
It's forced to follow. This is essentially how gravity works in Einstein's view

03:07.480 --> 03:13.760
Einstein's theory of relativity also explained anomalies that had left scientists scratching their heads for years

03:14.240 --> 03:17.820
Take for example the peculiar motion of the planet Mercury

03:18.200 --> 03:24.960
its orbit around the Sun shifts slightly each time a phenomenon known as the precession of the perihelion

03:25.840 --> 03:28.040
Newton's laws couldn't account for this shift

03:28.160 --> 03:34.800
But Einstein's theory hit the nail on the head the Sun's massive gravity was warping the space-time around Mercury

03:35.120 --> 03:38.000
Causing the planet to move in a slightly altered path

03:38.600 --> 03:41.860
This was a game-changing moment in the history of science

03:42.640 --> 03:47.000
The theory of relativity was not only groundbreaking. It was also testable

03:47.760 --> 03:54.880
during a solar eclipse in 1919 British astronomer Arthur Eddington observed the bending of starlight around the Sun a

03:55.120 --> 03:57.200
result predicted by Einstein's theory

03:58.040 --> 04:01.600
The observation was a success and Einstein's theory was confirmed

04:02.240 --> 04:03.840
through Einstein's

04:03.840 --> 04:10.160
Revolutionary theory gravity was no longer a mysterious force, but a predictable consequence of the curvature of space-time

04:11.000 --> 04:15.580
From then on we began to see the universe not as a stage where forces act

04:15.680 --> 04:23.440
But as a dynamic entity where mass and energy shaped the very fabric of reality from ancient philosophers to modern physicists

04:23.480 --> 04:27.480
Our understanding of gravity has come a long way at first

04:27.800 --> 04:33.920
Gravity was a perplexing concept a mysterious force that kept us grounded and the stars in their celestial dance

04:34.400 --> 04:37.840
But as time passed we began to unlock its secrets

04:38.360 --> 04:45.840
Sir Isaac Newton gave us a major breakthrough in the 17th century defining gravity as a predictable force between two objects

04:46.240 --> 04:52.800
However, it was Albert Einstein who turned our understanding of gravity on its head in the early 20th century

04:53.720 --> 04:56.140
He introduced us to the concept of space-time

04:56.840 --> 05:01.440
Painting gravity not as a force but as the curvature of space around an object

05:02.160 --> 05:05.420
Yet our comprehension of gravity is far from complete

05:06.120 --> 05:11.880
Theories like quantum gravity are being explored promising to further revolutionize our understanding

05:12.320 --> 05:20.040
While we now have a much better understanding of gravity. There are still many mysteries left to unravel the journey to fully understand gravity

05:20.480 --> 05:22.480
continues

